<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        x:Class="CcpClient.Desktop.Views.SessionEditorWindow"
        Title="Session Editor"
        Width="560" Height="560"
        CanResize="False"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
        Background="#FF141018">
  <!--
    SESSION EDITOR — upstream's Windows/SessionEditorWindow.xaml(.cs), opened from the rack row's
    edit action (MainWindow/MainWindow.SessionIO.cs:538 builds the button, :1819-1868 handles it).

    WHAT IS HERE IS THE THREE FIELDS UPSTREAM BINDS TO CONTROLS DIRECTLY, and no more: the name
    (SessionEditorWindow.xaml:80), the duration slider (:109-112) and the description
    (:234-239). Upstream's constructor loads exactly those three out of a session it is handed
    (SessionEditorWindow.xaml.cs:64-66) and its save reads exactly those three back (:1141-1142,
    plus the slider's own handler at :1027).

    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE is the rest of that window, and it is most of it: the drag-and-drop
    TIMELINE (:119-148), the twenty feature icons that drag onto it (:150-196), the per-feature
    settings popup (:251-255), the four-step tutorial overlay (:257-381), and Import/Export
    (:215-218). They are one feature — authoring a session's timeline — and this port models no
    timeline at all: ScriptedSession carries the FLATTENED Settings the four shipped files write,
    and nothing here can author, draw or persist a segment. The window says so on screen rather than
    only in this comment, which is the port's standing rule for a refusal (§9 D7).

    NOT A DIALOG. Upstream is modal (editor.ShowDialog(), MainWindow.SessionIO.cs:1828) and this is
    not: the port's own window convention is Show(owner) with the launch site keeping exactly one
    alive (Navigation/SessionRecapLaunch.cs, Navigation/LoomLaunch.cs), because nothing in this
    shell blocks on a window's result and an async ShowDialog inside a click handler would be the
    async-void shape the async-lifecycle contract bans. The consequence is real and bounded: the
    rack stays live behind this window, so a user can start a session while editing one. Nothing
    breaks if they do — a run holds its own ScriptedSession instance, and an edit of a built-in
    produces a COPY, so neither the running object nor the shipped file is what the save touches.
  -->
  <Window.Styles>
    <Style Selector="Border.plate">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="#FF241E2A" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#FF3A2F3E" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.field-label">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FFE8E0EE" />
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.7" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,4" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBox.field">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="#FF1B1620" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FFE8E0EE" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#FF3A2F3E" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="4" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,6" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.editor-action">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="#FF2A2130" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FFE8E0EE" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#FFD05CE8" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="16,8" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.editor-danger">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#FFF23557" />
    </Style>
  </Window.Styles>

  <DockPanel Margin="20">
    <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Top" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="0,0,0,12">
      <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" x:Name="Header"
                 AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorHeader"
                 Foreground="#FFD05CE8" FontSize="20" FontWeight="Bold"
                 Text="Session Editor" />
      <!-- THE PROVENANCE LINE, and it is load-bearing rather than decorative: editing a built-in
           produces a second session with the SAME NAME unless the user changes it, so the one
           question this window has to answer before anything else is "am I about to change the
           shipped session or make my own copy of it". Upstream answers it on the rack row instead,
           with a YOURS / BUILT-IN pill (MainWindow/MainWindow.SessionIO.cs:508-517, :588-597); the
           port now carries that badge on the row too, and this line is the same fact stated where
           the decision is being made. -->
      <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" x:Name="ProvenanceLine"
                 AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorProvenance"
                 Foreground="#FFE8E0EE" Opacity="0.7" FontSize="12"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center" TextAlignment="Right" Text="" />
    </Grid>

    <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Spacing="8" Margin="0,12,0,0">
      <TextBlock x:Name="RefusalLine"
                 AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorRefusal"
                 Foreground="#FFF23557" FontSize="12" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 IsVisible="False" Text="" />
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
        <!-- DELETE is upstream's rack action rather than an editor button
             (MainWindow/MainWindow.SessionIO.cs:540-544, offered only for a session that is not
             built-in, and :1946-1960 confirms first). It is HERE because this window is the only
             surface the port gives a custom session, and without it the editor would be a one-way
             door: a user could create their own sessions and could never remove one except through
             the file system. The confirmation is the caption's own second gesture rather than a
             third dialog idiom — the rack's confirmation strip already establishes the rule the
             ceremony has to keep, which is that a destructive act takes two separate gestures. -->
        <Button x:Name="DeleteButton"
                Classes="editor-action editor-danger"
                IsVisible="False"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorDeleteButton"
                AutomationProperties.Name="Delete"
                Content="Delete" />
        <Button x:Name="CancelButton"
                Classes="editor-action"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorCancelButton"
                AutomationProperties.Name="Cancel"
                Content="Cancel" />
        <Button x:Name="SaveButton"
                Classes="editor-action"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorSaveButton"
                AutomationProperties.Name="Save Session"
                Content="Save Session" />
      </StackPanel>
    </StackPanel>

    <Border Classes="plate">
      <StackPanel Spacing="12">
        <StackPanel>
          <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="Name" />
          <TextBox x:Name="NameBox"
                   Classes="field"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorNameBox"
                   AutomationProperties.Name="Session name"
                   Text="" />
        </StackPanel>

        <StackPanel>
          <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="Duration" />
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
            <!-- Upstream's slider, with upstream's bounds and upstream's snap
                 (SessionEditorWindow.xaml:109-112). The numbers are read off SessionEditorRules so
                 the control and the clamp cannot drift apart. -->
            <Slider Grid.Column="0" x:Name="DurationSlider"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorDurationSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Session duration in minutes" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" x:Name="DurationValue"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorDurationValue"
                       Foreground="#FFE8E0EE" FontSize="12" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="10,0,0,0" MinWidth="60"
                       TextAlignment="Right" Text="" />
          </Grid>
        </StackPanel>

        <StackPanel>
          <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="Description" />
          <!-- MULTI-LINE AND DELIBERATELY NOT WRAPPED, and the reason is measured rather than
               aesthetic. Upstream's box wraps (SessionEditorWindow.xaml:235).

               THE TRIGGER IS A BLANK LINE, NOT LENGTH — and the first record of this said length,
               which was wrong. Settled 2026-08-25 by reproducing it outside the suite. Under the
               HEADLESS TEST HARNESS ONLY, a wrapped Avalonia TextLayout over text containing an
               EMPTY LINE never terminates: the formatter returns a zero-length line at the blank
               line's position with no end-of-paragraph break, TextLayout's own loop advances by
               that length, and it appends a line per iteration until the process is past 19 GB.
               Every shipped session description contains "\n\n", which is why opening this editor
               was the thing that hung. Wrapping 160 characters with no newline is fine (14 lines,
               0.42 s); a LEADING newline is enough to hang it; a TRAILING one is not;
               WrapWithOverflow hangs too; NoWrap never reaches that code path at all.

               AND IT IS THE HARNESS, NOT THIS PRODUCT. The same text and the same wrap width
               formats in 0.42 s against Skia's real system fonts and in 0.40 s under headless stub
               DRAWING with a real embedded font. Only Avalonia.Headless.BareMinimum.ttf — the font
               the headless platform serves, which nothing shipped ever uses — reaches the loop. So
               a real user on a real desktop would get correct wrapping here.

               NoWrap ships anyway, and this is the honest trade rather than a defect: the port's
               evidence base is the headless suite, and a field it cannot open is worth less than a
               field that scrolls sideways. The box still accepts newlines, so authored paragraphs
               are still authored, and the rack row shows the first line either way. Recorded as a
               divergence, and it is now a divergence with an EXIT: the artefact is pinned by
               CcpClient.HeadlessTests/HeadlessWrappedTextArtefactTests.cs, which reds the moment it
               stops being true and says to restore this attribute when it does. -->
          <TextBox x:Name="DescriptionBox"
                   Classes="field"
                   Height="110"
                   AcceptsReturn="True"
                   TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                   ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                   ScrollViewer.VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorDescriptionBox"
                   AutomationProperties.Name="Session description"
                   Text="" />
          <TextBlock Foreground="#FFE8E0EE" Opacity="0.6" FontSize="11" Margin="0,4,0,0"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     Text="The rack row shows the first line; the whole thing is on its tooltip." />
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- WHAT THIS EDITOR DOES NOT EDIT, said where the user is rather than only in a comment.
             §9 D7's rule: absent rather than greyed, and NAMED rather than silently missing. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="AbsenceLine"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionEditorAbsence"
                   Foreground="#FFE8E0EE" Opacity="0.6" FontSize="11"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>
  </DockPanel>
</Window>
